TEECLI
Test Execution Environment for Cross-Language Implementations
Executable specifications for cross-language parity.
One specification. One test suite. Many implementations.

TEECLI (pronounced TEAK-lee) is a framework for using a single suite of
executable specifications to verify the behavioral parity of multiple
implementations of a library in different languages.  Any natural
language description of the desired behavior is considered to be only an
aid and explanation, and we assume that the automated test suite is the
formal specification.  By using small VMs to make this specification
executable across multiple languages, rigorous conformance testing of
every implementation is possible using only a single common test suite,
and there is no room for differing interpretations of the specification.
The architecture is modular so that additional languages may be added,
and a library need not be modified to be tested.  TEECLI is open-sourced
under the LGPL, is available on https://github.com/mkenniston/TEECLI,
and runs under Linux.

For more detailed information, refer to the TEECLI manual.

